---
id: 9111e931-2764-4736-9dc8-7be96803c8b8
title: Ankylosing Spondylitis
---

# Ankylosing Spondylitis

- **Definition** - Seronegative spondyloarthropathy characterised by chronic inflammatory arthritis predominantly affecting sacroiliac joints and spine, which can cause ankylosis (bone fusion)
- **Epidemiology:**
  - <u>Prevalence</u> - \< 0.5% population
  - <u>Demographic</u> - onset typically between 20-30y, male preponderance (M:F = 3:1)
  - <u>Burden</u> - 75% of patient remain in employment with good Mx (even if ankylosis occurs, as long as spine is fused in erect position)
- **Pathophysiology** - ill-defined interation between environmental pathogens, host immune system in genetically susceptible individuals:
  - <u>HLA-B27 association</u> - 3 proposed theories:
    - Antigen presentation - B27 presents arthritogenic or enthesitogenic peptide to cytotoxic T cells
    - Protein misfold - causes ER stress, leading to release of inflammatory cytokines
    - Propensity to form homodimers - presentation to leukocytes
- **Clinical features of AS:**
  - **HLA-B27 positive** - present in \> 90% of AS patients (although non-sensitive nor-specific to warrant role in diagnosis)
  - **Sacroiliitis and inflammatory spondylitis** - predominant axial involvement is characteristic of AS:
    - **Low back pain** - inflammatory pain where characteristic features as defined in Dx criteria:
      - <u>Site</u> - lower back, with slow ascension along the spine (ocassionally presents initially in cervical or thoracic spine)
      - <u>Onset</u> - insiduous onset and chronic (\> 3 mo), and onset \< 40y
      - <u>Progression</u> - progressively increasing pain, w/ slow ascension along the spine (late feature)
      - <u>Quality</u> - inflammatory type pain, not relieved by rest and occurs at night
      - <u>Radiation</u> - ocassionally to buttocks or posterior thigh
      - <u>Exacerbating and relieving factors</u>:
        - Relieved by exercise
        - Exacerbated by rest or prolonged inactivity
      - <u>Timing</u> - characteristically **night pain**, resulting in reduced sleep quality
    - **Early morning stiffness** - stiffness of the back after long periods of inactivity
    - **Progressive ankylosis** - due to disease progression resulting in ankylosis, ocassionally causing severe thoracic and cervical kyphosis (patient unable to see the sun)
    - **Vertebral fractures** - secondary osteoporosis of the vertebral bodies frequently occur, leading to an increased risk of vertebral fractures
    - **Pleuritic chest pain** - due to costovertebral joint involvement
  - **Peripheral arthritis** (40%) - asymetrical oligoarthritis typically affecting large joints:
    - <u>Sites</u> - Hips, knees, ankles and shoulders
    - <u>Onset</u> - only 10% peripheral arthritis antedate spinal Sx, where in a further 10% (1% of all) manifest as JIA from childhood
  - **Features of extra-synovial inflammation:**
    - <u>Enthesitis</u> - manifests as plantar fasciitis, achilles tendinitis
    - <u>Inflammation over bone</u> - tenderness over bony prominence (e.g. iliac crest and greater trochanter) at insertion of tendons
  - **Extra-articular manifestations:**
    - <u>Fatigue</u> - often a predominant complaint and may result from chronic interruption of sleep due to pain, chronic systemic inflammation with direct effects of inflammotory cytokines on the brain
    - <u>Acute anterior uveitis</u> (25%) - ocassionally precedes joint disease, painful red eye w/ blurred vision and photophobia due to inflammation of the iris and anterior ciliary body

    
    ![](../img/extra_articular_features_of_ankylosing_spondylitis.png)
- **Signs of AS:**
  - Reduced lumbar spine motion in all directions:
    - Modified Schober's Test - maximal flexion
    - Lateral spinal flexion
    - Occipital to Wall and Tragus to Wall
    - Cervical rotation
  - Pain on sacroiliac stressing
- **Ix:**
  - **Imaging**:
    - <u>Radiographs</u> - over hip, lateeral thoracolumbar spine and C-spine
    - <u>MRI</u> - earlier detection of sacroiliitis than X-ray and allow for detection of early axial spondyloarthritis
  - **Blood tests** - inflammatory markers, serologies:
    - <u>Inflammatory markers</u> (ESR, CRP) - elevated in active disease but may be normal
    - <u>Other serologies</u> (e.g. RF, ANCA) - -ve results
    - <u>HLA-B27 testing</u> - helpful but non-diagnostic
- **Radiographic features of Ankylosing spondylitis** - early disease may be associated w/ normal radiographs, and MRI should be pursued if clinical suspicion is high:
  - <u>Sacroiliac joints</u> (SI joints) - demonstration of presence of sacroiliiitis +/- ankylosis:
    - Grade 2 - irregular joint line and loss of cortical margins (+/- narrowing joint space and sclerosis)
    - Grade 3 - partial fusion of SI joint
    - Grade 4 - complete fusion of SI joint

    
    ![](../img/radiological_sacroiliitis.png)
  - <u>Lateral thoracolumbar films</u>:
    - Squaring of the vertebrae (due to erosion or sclerosis of the anterior corners)
    - Bridging syndesmophytes - areas of calcification that follow the outermost fibres of the anulus
  - <u>Lumbar spine</u> - demonstration of bone erosion, syndesmophyte formation, and subsequent ankylosis:
    - Bone erosion - generalised osteopenia
    - Syndesmophyte formation - calcification of the outermost fibres of the annulus
    - Ossification of anterior longitudinal ligament +/- facet joint function
    - Bamboo shoot appearence - characterised by the aformentioned features

    ![](../img/radiograph_AS_spine_1.png) 
    ![](../img/radiograph_AS_spine_2.png)
- **MRI features of early Ankylosing spondylitis** - Short T1 inversion recovery (STIR) sequence (more sensitive)
- **Diagnositic criteria of AS** - Modified New York Criteria:
  - Definite AS - 1 radiological criteria + 1 clinical criteria
  - Radiological criteria - Grade 2 sacroiliitis bilaterally or Grade 3-4 sacroiliitis unilaterally
  - Clinical criteria:
    - Characteristic inflammatory back pain \> 3mo, improved by exercise and not relieved by rest
    - Limitations of lumbar spine movement in sagittal and frontal planes
    - Chest expansion reduced
  - Limitations - radiological features delayed by 6-11 y
- **Diagnostic criteria of Axial Spondyloarthritis** - ASAS classification criteria:
  - Patients w/ characteristic back pain with 1) improved by exercise, 2) not relieved by rest, 3) night pain, and 4) age of onset \< 40y - Diagnosis w/ Sacroillitis on imaging + 1 other clinical feature, or HLA positive with \>=2 clinical features in absence of sacroiliitis
  - Patients w/ peripheral Sx (arthritis, enthesitis, dactylitis) - requires combination with other clinical features

  
  ![](../img/axial_SpA_diagnosis.png)
- **Disease activity score** - Bath Ankylosing Spondylitis Disease Activity Index: 
![](../img/BASDAI.png)
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx:**
    - **Goals of Mx:**
      - <u>Symptomatic relief</u> - relieve pain and stiffness
      - <u>Retain functional status</u> - maintain maximal ROM and avoid development of deformities
    - **Approach to therapy:**
      - <u>General measures</u> - patient education, analgesics
      - <u>Analgesics</u> - long acting NSAIDs and analgesia for effective alleviation of pain and morning stiffness
      - <u>Systemic therapy</u> - biologics (anti-TNF-alpha), and various antimetabolites (e.g. methotrexate, sulfasalazine), systemic corticosteroids
      - <u>Local therapy</u> - local corticosteroid injections effective for peripheral arthritis or enthesitis
      - <u>Surgery</u> - useful if advanced deformity
  - **Patient education:**
    - <u>Regular exercise and stretching</u> - as pain is characteristically relieved by exercise:
      - Daily back extension exercise as morning 'warm-up' routine
      - Swimming as the ideal exercise
    - <u>Reduce imobility</u> - punctate periods of inactivity with regular breaks
    - <u>Posture</u> - poor posture must be avoided to avoid pain and potentially subsequent deformity
  - **Anti-TNF-alpha** - indicated for poor Sx control on standard therapy (BASDAI score \> 4):
    - <u>Selection</u> - e.g. adalimumab, infliximab
    - <u>Clinical efficacy</u>:
      - Effective Sx control (especially for axial disease and extra-articular manifestations \[N.B. use in IBD\])
      - No role in slowing disease progression, altering natural Hx and preventing ankylosis
